WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.627
Episode three filled the library.

2
00:00:03.627 --> 00:00:05.540
Now we hire the librarian.

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:11.645
In this episode: your first agent, built
end to end — and why the smallest agent is

4
00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:13.190
usually the best one.

5
00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:15.901
Agents live here.

6
00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:20.670
You met the built-in ones in episode
one — the Assistant, the Researcher.

7
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:25.374
Today we add our own: a coach that
helps the support team draft replies.

8
00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:29.733
An agent starts with a
name and an identity.

9
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:34.615
Create agent, blank, a technical
name… a display name… and continue.

10
00:00:34.615 --> 00:00:39.711
That is the whole ceremony — the
real work is the four decisions ahead.

11
00:00:43.574 --> 00:00:47.386
Decision one: the
instructions — the job description.

12
00:00:47.386 --> 00:00:50.623
Tone, duties, and the
line it must not cross.

13
00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:55.946
Ours says: be friendly and concrete,
and hand billing disputes to a human.

14
00:00:55.946 --> 00:00:59.254
Write it like you would
brief a new colleague.

15
00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:02.691
Decision two: what it may read.

16
00:01:02.691 --> 00:01:08.427
The knowledge scope from last episode
applies per agent — give the coach the support

17
00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:10.271
documents and nothing more.

18
00:01:10.271 --> 00:01:12.934
The smallest library that does the job.

19
00:01:14.134 --> 00:01:17.458
Decision three is the
one to respect: tools.

20
00:01:17.458 --> 00:01:22.443
Every box you tick widens what
this agent can DO — search the web,

21
00:01:22.443 --> 00:01:25.918
write files, run code,
call your integrations.

22
00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:28.033
Capability is also exposure.

23
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:32.414
Start with none, add each
tool the day the job demands it.

24
00:01:33.614 --> 00:01:35.341
Decision four: the engine.

25
00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:40.787
Pick a model, add a fallback for busy
days — or leave it on the workspace default.

26
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:44.173
For a drafting coach, the
default is exactly right.

27
00:01:45.373 --> 00:01:49.610
Make it visible in chat, and the
whole team has a new colleague.

28
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:54.907
We test it right away: a customer wants
an invoice copy… and look at the answer.

29
00:01:54.907 --> 00:01:59.211
Friendly, concrete — and it hands
the billing dispute to a human,

30
00:01:59.211 --> 00:02:00.667
exactly as instructed.

31
00:02:07.373 --> 00:02:10.765
An agent is a draft you
refine, not a monument.

32
00:02:10.765 --> 00:02:16.466
Instructions are cheap to change, and
the history keeps every version — improve

33
00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:18.270
boldly, roll back freely.

34
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.867
Name, instructions, knowledge,
tools, model — a colleague you designed,

35
00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:27.452
scoped as small as the job allows.

36
00:02:27.452 --> 00:02:32.621
Next episode: automations — agents
that act on schedules and events,

37
00:02:32.621 --> 00:02:35.510
with people approving the risky steps.

38
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:41.649
The agents section of the
documentation walks every decision you just made.

39
00:02:41.649 --> 00:02:43.230
See you in episode five.

